abstract:
This article describes the system submitted by the RGCL team to the IDAT 2019&#13;
Shared Task: Irony Detection in Arabic Tweets. The system detects irony in Arabic tweets using&#13;
deep learning. The paper evaluates the performance of several deep learning models, as well as&#13;
how text cleaning and text pre-processing influence the accuracy of the system. Several runs&#13;
were submitted. The highest F1 score achieved for one of the submissions was 0.818 making the&#13;
team RGCL rank 4th out of 10 teams in final results. Overall, we present a system that uses&#13;
minimal pre-processing but capable of achieving competitive results.

Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) have recently emerged as a powerful paradigm for multimodal artificial intelligence, enabling unified understanding and generation across visual and linguistic modalities. By leveraging large-scale pretraining on image-text pairs and adopting transformer-based architectures, LVLMs have demonstrated impressive capabilities in a wide range of tasks, including visual question answering, image captioning, multimodal reasoning, and interactive dialogue. However, these advances come at the cost of extremely high computational complexity, memory consumption, energy usage, and data requirements, which significantly hinder their scalability, reproducibility, and real-world deployment. As a result, improving the efficiency of LVLMs has become a critical research direction, essential for enabling practical applications on resource-constrained platforms and for promoting sustainable development of multimodal AI systems.&amp;nbsp;
&lt;div&gt;
 This survey provides a comprehensive and systematic review of research on efficient Large Vision-Language Models. We begin by formalizing the fundamental components of LVLMs and analyzing the intrinsic sources of inefficiency arising from large model scale, high-dimensional visual inputs, long token sequences, and expensive cross-modal interactions. We then identify and discuss key efficiency challenges that span the entire model lifecycle, including pretraining cost, fine-tuning overhead, inference latency, memory footprint, and evaluation complexity. To structure the rapidly growing literature, we propose a unified taxonomy of efficiency techniques, categorizing existing methods by their stage of application, targeted computational resources, and level of intervention, ranging from architectural design and training strategies to deployment-time optimization.&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 Building on this taxonomy, we review representative approaches for efficient LVLMs, including lightweight and modular architectures, frozenbackbone training, knowledge distillation, parameter-efficient fine-tuning, sparse and conditional computation, as well as model compression techniques such as quantization and pruning. We analyze the core ideas, advantages, and limitations of these methods, highlighting the trade-offs between efficiency, performance, adaptability, and robustness. In addition, we survey existing evaluation protocols and benchmarks, emphasizing&amp;nbsp;accuracy-based evaluation and better reflect real-world deployment constraints.&amp;nbsp;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 Finally, we discuss practical application scenarios where efficiency is crucial, such as on-device intelligence, real-time interactive systems, autonomous and embodied agents, and large-scale industrial deployment. We conclude by outlining open challenges and future research directions, including understanding the relationship between efficiency and emergent multimodal capabilities, improving data- and compute-efficient pretraining, designing adaptive and hardware-aware LVLMs, and developing standardized evaluation frameworks. Overall, this survey aims to provide a comprehensive reference and conceptual foundation for researchers and practitioners working toward LVLMs that are not only powerful, but also efficient, scalable, and sustainable.
&lt;/div&gt;
